erything in  high energy and particle physics  practically shared with  large community of tens of thousands of  physicists who can look at the data and  eventually they all publish their  results together they have very  stringent statistical rules for claiming  discoveries so they have a 5 Sigma rule  that is equivalent to a p-value of 10 to  the minus 7 which is very close to the  genome wide level of significance that  is used in biomedicine that has worked  pretty well in in genetic Association  studies for example so there's many  reasons why you see a pretty good  reproducibility in these Sciences  there's also great tolerance for  negative results most of the results in  experimental physics are negative  practically and nobody where is about  that you know the you see all these  papers coming out from CERN hundreds of  papers they have found one Higgs boson  and pretty much nothing else there was a  great discovery but I would say it's an  equally great discovery that all the  other results are negative and people  just say that this is what they are  there are still some problems in  specific fields that the experimentation  or the observation is more difficult for  example claims for exoplanets about 50%  of the time they turn out to be non  reproducible range from 25% to 60% in  different surveys some of the more  recent wave of chemical syntheses have  been a bit difficult to reproduce  independent labs but I hope that this is  the exception  so no scientific field is immune to this  problems because these are not  necessarily problems this is the  scientific method and you know and it's  difficult to do good science that's  great and on that note that we should we  should end this reached the end of the  hour John let me thank you for was a  really stimulating talk and well let me  thank all the people who dialed in this  morning was really great to hear you  thanks again thank you
 

